<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pátzcuaro</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pátzcuaro is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, near the southern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a>. It was one of the early capitals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> and a sacred site, and after the Spanish conquest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-de-quiroga">Vasco de Quiroga</a> made it the seat of the diocese of Michoacán in 1538 and promoted craft villages around the lake. Its colonial centre is well preserved.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patzcuaro.jpg" alt="Pátzcuaro" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gibranlp · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunset_P%C3%A1tzcuaro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pátzcuaro is a city and municipality in the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a>, on the southern edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a>, which still has economic and cultural importance for the town. The lake holds several islands, the best known being Janitzio, topped by a forty-metre statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos y Pavón</a>. Several possible meanings of the name are given, among them &quot;place of foundations&quot;, &quot;place of bullrushes&quot; and &quot;seat of temples&quot;. The municipality covers most of the Pátzcuaro basin, within the Trans-Mexican Volcanic Belt, and has a total population of 79,868, including 104 other named communities besides the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest written account of the founding is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relacion-de-michoacan">Relación de Michoacán</a>, written in 1539 to 1541. It says two chiefs, Páracume and Vápeani, arrived with their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca">Chichimeca</a> people and began building temples by placing four large rocks together. No date is given, but as the chiefs died in 1360, the founding is widely supposed to be around 1324. Tariácuri, the first chief to take the title caltzontzin, or emperor, by conquering his neighbours, began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire</a>, which was divided into three principalities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ihuatzio">Ihuatzio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a> and Pátzcuaro. Power later shifted to Tzintzuntzan, leaving Pátzcuaro as a ceremonial centre and retreat for the nobility.</p><p>When the Spanish arrived, Cristóbal de Olid forced the emperor Tanganxoan II to kneel in front of a crowd, and a chapel later built there is called El Humilladero, the Place of Humiliation. In 1526 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-de-guzman">Nuño de Guzmán</a> came to punish the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> harshly, ending in the torture and death of Tanganxoan II, and most residents fled to the mountains. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-de-quiroga">Vasco de Quiroga</a> then expelled Guzmán.</p><p>In 1538 the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a> was founded with Quiroga as first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and Pátzcuaro became the capital of the new province. He persuaded many Indians to return and brought Spanish families, and is considered the founder of modern Pátzcuaro. Its coat of arms came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1553. The cathedral was built over a temple of the goddess Cueráppari. In 1575 the viceregal authorities moved the capital to Valladolid, today Morelia, and the diocese followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pátzcuaro keeps its colonial and indigenous character, with adobe or wooden houses, tiled roofs and cobblestone streets, and has been named one of the 111 Pueblos Mágicos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. The Plaza Vasco de Quiroga is the centre, and the Basilica of Nuestra Señora de la Salud, designated a basilica in 1924, holds the remains of Quiroga. The Palace of Huitziméngari belonged to the son of the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> cazonci. The town is the market hub of the lake region, whose villages make copperware, black pottery, musical instruments and baskets. The surrounding lake region is well known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/day-of-the-dead">Day of the Dead</a> celebrations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pátzcuaro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the fourteenth century their leader Tariácuri, lord of <b>Pátzcuaro</b>, brought the lake communities under his control through war and alliance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
